  "live_records": {
    "q-59740": {
      "dom_id": "step-q59740",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199778/assets/3865ec425991f0d8ceae82f002d0096f2fd2ea56743173e754b15472b7c85031.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}$ hexagon.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $3$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $3$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $1$ hexagon.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
